   "text": "This SMILES was read from the drawing, not checked with software. It describes a bicyclic peptide on a triazinane (TATA-type) scaffold, with a BCN-triazole at the C-terminal lysine and a phosphate linker ending in an open attachment point.\n\n**Peptide sequence**\n- The sequence runs from the N-terminus: Ac-Cys1, Pro, Hyp, Asp, Ala, Tyr, Leu, Gly, Cys2, tert-butyl-Gly, Ser, Tyr, Cys3, Glu, Pro, Trp, Lys-NH2.\n- Hyp is 4-hydroxyproline.\n- I wrote all residues as the natural L-forms. For Cys1 and Cys2 I checked the wedge/hash geometry and it comes out as R, which is L-cysteine. I did not check the other residues individually.\n\n**Scaffold**\n- The three cysteine sulfurs are each linked through S\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O) to the three nitrogens of a hexahydro-1,3,5-triazine ring.\n\n**C-terminal lysine**\n- The lysine side chain ends in a 1,2,3-triazole fused to a cyclooctene, which is also fused to a cyclopropane. This is a BCN-type unit.\n- The cyclopropane CH2\u2013O is hashed and both ring-fusion H atoms are wedged, so I assigned stereo from that geometry. It is the endo form, with ring-fusion stereo C@, C@@, C@ in the order written.\n\n**Linker**\n- The BCN CH2\u2013O is a carbamate to NH\u2013(CH2)3\u2013CH(CH2OH)\u2013CH2\u2013O\u2013P(=O)(OH)\u2013, ending at the wavy bond. I counted three CH2 groups between NH and the branch carbon by estimating bond lengths in the drawing. This is the least certain part of the reading.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)N[C@@H](CSCC(=O)N1CN(C(=O)CS%11)CN(C(=O)CS%12)C1)C(=O)N1CCC[C@H]1C(=O)N1C[C@H](O)C[C@H]1C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccc(O)cc1)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@@H](C%11)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](CO)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccc(O)cc1)C(=O)N[C@@H](C%12)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCC(=O)O)C(=O)N1CCC[C@H]1C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]c2ccccc12)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCCn1nnc2c1CC[C@H]3[C@@H](CC2)[C@H]3COC(=O)NCCCC(CO)COP(=O)(O)*)C(N)=O"
